Q2. Explain Dependency Inversion Principle
using PaymentService.

Interview Ready Answer

Without DIP

class PaymentService{

    StripePayment payment=new StripePayment();

    void makePayment(){

        payment.pay();
    }
}

Problem

PaymentService directly depends on
StripePayment.

If Stripe is replaced with Razorpay,

PaymentService must be modified.

This creates

Tight Coupling.

------------------------------------------------------------

With DIP

interface Payment{

    void pay();
}

------------------------------------------------------------

class StripePayment implements Payment{

    public void pay(){

        System.out.println("Stripe Payment");
    }
}

------------------------------------------------------------

class RazorpayPayment implements Payment{

    public void pay(){

        System.out.println("Razorpay Payment");
    }
}

------------------------------------------------------------

class PaymentService{

    private Payment payment;

    PaymentService(Payment payment){

        this.payment=payment;
    }

    void makePayment(){

        payment.pay();
    }
}

------------------------------------------------------------

Client Code

Payment payment=new StripePayment();

PaymentService service=
new PaymentService(payment);

service.makePayment();

------------------------------------------------------------

Later

Payment payment=new RazorpayPayment();

PaymentService service=
new PaymentService(payment);

service.makePayment();

PaymentService remains unchanged.

Only the implementation changes.

This is Dependency Inversion Principle.

